title: The undo selection
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
summary: What undo and redo choose — the newest change you made, or the newest undo, still standing — computed from the log alone, in core, by every client; both are reverts.

Both are reverts, applied onto the current state ([[Reverting onto the current state]]):
nothing moves back over the log, so nothing written since — the watcher's work above all
— is lost or left on a dead branch ([[Why the log stops being a tree]]).

!! Undo

Undo walks the log newest first for the newest revision ''the user wrote'' that still
stands, and reverts it. A revision is ''not'' a candidate when:

* it is the daemon's — `origin = watcher` exactly, not any set origin ([[Revisions and operations]]); failing that, when all its operations are watcher types. A `trash` revision ''is'' a candidate: you asked for it;
* it is ''itself'' a revert — an undo or a redo; undoing it would be a redo, and treating it as a candidate would make undo a two-state oscillation;
* every one of its operations has already been undone;
* it holds only `unknown` operations, which carry nothing to invert;
* it holds the log's first operation — the root metarecord written at init, or the oldest operation a prune left (`first` in `GET /log`). Undo stops there; `mf log revert <id>` still reaches it.

A revision partly undone already — some operations reverted one by one — is undone by its
remaining operations.

!! Redo

Redo walks the log newest first for the newest ''undo'' that still stands — a revert of an
ordinary change — and reverts it. On the way it steps over the daemon's revisions, the
redos, and the undos already redone: pressing redo again goes on to the undo before. The
newest ordinary change still standing ends the search: ''a new change clears what could
be redone'', as in any editor. What it cleared is still in the log, and an explicit
`mf log revert` of the undo reaches it.

Undo, change, undo, undo — then redo, redo, redo: the first two redos bring back what the
last two undos took, the third finds the change and answers "Nothing to redo."; the
change undone before it stays undone ([[Why undo and redo do not oscillate]]).

!! Where it lives

The selection is a pure function of `GET /log`, in `core::undo`, shared by `mf log
undo`/`redo` and the GUI's `log:undo`/`log:redo`; the daemon has no undo endpoint — it
offers the revert, the client picks. Both read the log in two bounded passes — 500
operations, then 20 000 — rather than one unbounded one: after a large reconcile the log is
millions of rows, and a repository whose last manual change is older than the second
window is told so rather than scanned whole. The revert itself — including the files it
moves — is run by `core::navigation`, the same for both clients.
